<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Yi of Chu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/206-bce">206 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Xiong Xin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Yi of Chu, born Xiong Xin, was a grandson of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-huai-of-chu">King Huai of Chu</a> whom rebels against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> enthroned in 208 BCE under the same title, King Huai of Chu, as the figurehead of their rising. After the fall of Qin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> gave him the empty title of Emperor Yi, removed him to the south and had him murdered in 206 BCE.</p></div>
